One of the most significant advantages of TypeScript is its support for static typing. In JavaScript, variables can hold values of any type, which can lead to runtime errors if the code assumes a certain type but receives a different one. TypeScript allows you to define the types of variables, function parameters, and return values explicitly.
// JavaScript example
function add ( a , b ) {
return a + b;
}
// This can lead to unexpected results
console. log ( add ( 1 , '2' )); // Output: "12"
// TypeScript example
function addTyped ( a : number , b : number ) : number {
// This will cause a compilation error
// console.log(addTyped(1, '2'));
Type annotations in TypeScript act as documentation for your code. They make it clear what types of values are expected and returned by functions, which helps other developers understand the code more easily. This is especially important in large codebases where multiple developers are working on the same project.
// Function with type annotations
function getFullName ( firstName : string , lastName : string ) : string {
return `${ firstName } ${ lastName }` ;
// It's clear what this function expects and returns
Since TypeScript is a statically typed language, it can catch many errors at compile-time rather than at runtime. This means you can fix issues before the code is deployed, reducing the number of bugs in production.
// TypeScript will catch this error during compilation
let num : number ;
num = 'hello' ; // Compilation error: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'.
TypeScript has excellent tooling support. Integrated Development Environments (IDEs) like Visual Studio Code can provide intelligent code completion, refactoring support, and better error highlighting based on the type information in your code.
You can install TypeScript globally using npm (Node Package Manager):
npm install -g typescript
To configure TypeScript for your project, you need to create a tsconfig.json file in the root directory of your project. This file contains the compiler options for TypeScript.
"compilerOptions" : {
"target" : "ES6" ,
"module" : "commonjs" ,
"strict" : true ,
"outDir" : "./dist"
"include" : [ "src/**/*.ts" ]
Once you have written your TypeScript code and configured tsconfig.json , you can compile it using the tsc command.
tsc
This will compile all the TypeScript files in the src directory (as specified in tsconfig.json ) and output the JavaScript files in the dist directory.
